text:
alex: Quick Q — building env var keys with `printf` in a Helm tpl and seeing weird precedence when values are empty vs missing. snippet:

alex: ```yaml
{{- $env := .Values.env.globalName | default "" }}
{{- $name := printf "REDWOOD_%s_%s" .Values.service.name $env }}
- name: {{ $name }}
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: {{ .Values.secrets.appSecret | default "" }}
      key: api_key
```

alex: If `appSecret` is set to "" in a values override, the `name:` renders empty which breaks k8s. Should we treat empty string as unset? What's the idiomatic Helm guard?

lina: Short answer: yes, guard for empty string. Use `and` + `ne` or `hasKey`. Example:

lina: ```yaml
{{- if and .Values.secrets.appSecret (ne .Values.secrets.appSecret "") }}
- name: {{ printf "REDWOOD_%s_%s" .Values.service.name (.Values.env.globalName | default "") }}
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: {{ .Values.secrets.appSecret }}
      key: api_key
{{- end }}
```

lina: Or use `lookup` to verify secret exists at render time in cluster (helm v3+):

lina: ```gotemplate
{{- $s := lookup "v1" "Secret" .Release.Namespace .Values.secrets.appSecret }}
{{- if $s }}
  # secret exists, render binding
{{- end }}
```

ben: If you want override semantics where empty string = unset, normalize in `_helpers.tpl` and call the helper from values. Example helper:

ben: ```gotemplate
{{- define "helpers.normalizedSecret" -}}
{{- $v := .Values.secrets.appSecret -}}
{{- if and $v (ne $v "") -}}
  {{- $v -}}
{{- else -}}
  {{- "" -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}
```

ben: then use `{{ include "helpers.normalizedSecret" . }}` where you need the name.

alex: nice. I'm going to add a guard and a comment in the chart. Pinging @lina @ben — mind eyeballing my PR? https://github.com/redwood/infrastructure/pull/1234 :pray:

